# tests/harness/giveup.rxt — [DD-14 wave A commit 3] the `gu` directive's
# own positive corpus: two cells where a typed give-up code IS reachable
# today, with no new construct, through the `engine`/`budget` directives
# this commit also adds (docs/testing.md's "The `gu` directive").
#
# Lives here rather than under a module directory because it is testing
# HARNESS VOCABULARY (does `gu` correctly score a give-up), not any
# particular regex construct's behavior — the same reason run.sh/driver.c
# themselves live in tests/harness/ rather than under tests/base/.
#
# The two patterns and budgets below are the SAME cells
# tests/vm/run_vm_tests.sh's own `build()` calls use to drive each bound to
# its own limit (§4/§4.5 there): '(a*)*b' at --step-budget=50 for STEPS,
# '((a)|b)*c' at --backtrack-frames=4 for FRAMES. That file exercises them
# through its own C driver (`err_steps`/`err_frames`); this file exercises
# the identical mechanism through the .rxt corpus's own vocabulary, which
# before this commit had no way to assert a give-up at all (§10.3).

pattern (a*)*b
engine vm
budget steps=50
budget frames=4096
gu steps "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"

pattern ((a)|b)*c
engine vm
budget frames=4
gu frames "aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa"
